// The hairpin twin of test/rit-span-in-a-repeated-section: ONE written @cresc, in a section
// the form plays TWICE, with no dynamic to close it. Both playings must draw the same wedge.
// A form that lists a section twice draws it twice, so the mark becomes one MusicMarkItem per
// playing — same source position, different measures — and "the next cresc/decresc ends this
// one" found the second copy of itself. MEASURED 2026-08-29: played once the wedge is 7.58
// wide (scratch/p289/c7), played twice the first was 17.18 against the second's 7.58.
// ⚠️ NO DYNAMIC ANYWHERE, and that is the point: a dynamic in the same playing would close
// the wedge before the repeat could, which is exactly why the corpus never showed this.
octave absolute
title "hairpin in a repeated section"

part m { clef treble }

section A { m { c'2@cresc c'2 | c'2 c'2 | } }
section B { m { d'2 d'2 | d'2 d'2 | } }

form main { A B A "A2" }

score main { staff m }
